quare wave: V is +V₀ for the first half of each period T and −V₀ for the second half. Two periods are shown.'),
('b0e629f5-4276-5edd-99d5-75d0cdf2e05a', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 21, 21, 'A sinusoidal current has an r.m.s. value of 0.50 A. Calculate its peak value.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.7071', 'A', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'I₀ = √2 × 0.50 = 0.707 A. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: I₀ = 0.707 A.', 1, NULL, NULL),
('3ace58d0-aba8-5050-965b-6052ce93dea8', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 22, 22, 'Which expression gives the mean power dissipated in a resistor R by a sinusoidal current?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'I_rms²R', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Mean power = I_rms²R = ½I₀²R. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (I_rms²R).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('568802d6-b3bd-5019-bbce-def0653f74e3', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 23, 23, 'A sinusoidal current of peak value 2.0 A flows through a 10 Ω resistor. Calculate the mean power.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '20', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P = ½I₀²R = 0.5 × 2.0² × 10 = 20 W. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: ½I₀²R. A1: P = 20 W.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('d8804c2b-8384-51be-8a96-555e4e775e9a', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 24, 24, 'How does the instantaneous power in a resistor connected to a sinusoidal supply vary?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Between zero and twice the mean power', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P = I₀²R sin²ωt varies from 0 to I₀²R = 2P_mean. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (Between zero and twice the mean power).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('fb31f983-c075-58c0-bd8c-7b4a540d4545', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 25, 25, 'A lamp of resistance 240 Ω dissipates a mean power of 60 W on an a.c. supply. Calculate the r.m.s. voltage across it.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '120', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'V_rms = √(PR) = √(60 × 240) = 120 V. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: P = V_rms²/R. C1: rearranged. A1: V_rms = 120 V.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('b672b42c-c937-51c4-b208-e524458f7cde', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 26, 26, 'An r.m.s. current of 2.0 A flows through a 15 Ω resistor. Calculate the peak voltage across it.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '42.43', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'V_rms = 2.0 × 15 = 30 V. V₀ = √2 × 30 = 42.4 V. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: V_rms = I_rms R. C1: × √2. A1: V₀ = 42.4 V.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('4914f17d-9839-51cb-b46f-531c50fdc962', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 27, 27, 'A resistor is connected first to a 10 V d.c. supply and then to a sinusoidal supply of peak 10 V. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The d.c. supply gives twice the mean power', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The a.c. has V_rms = 7.07 V, so P is (7.07/10)² = ½ of the d.c. power. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: V_rms = V₀/√2. C1: P ∝ V². A1: C (The d.c. supply gives twice the mean power).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgbcc33057-t fgbcc33057-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgbcc33057-t">d.c. and a.c. supplies</title><desc id="fgbcc33057-d">Graph of p.d. against time t. A horizontal dashed line shows the steady 10 V d.c. supply; a sine curve of peak 10 V shows the a.c. supply, which reaches 10 V only at its peaks.</desc><line x1="36.2" y1="138" x2="388" y2="138" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.2" y1="242" x2="36.2" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,138 385.7,141 385.7,135" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="36.2,24 39.1,30.3 33.2,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="36.2" y1="138" x2="36.2" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="36.2" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="209.1" y1="138" x2="209.1" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke