('45e708ae-745a-5be8-8ae6-757e3602318c', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 30, 30, 'The peak voltage of a sinusoidal supply across a resistor is doubled. What happens to the r.m.s. voltage and to the mean power?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The r.m.s. voltage doubles and the mean power quadruples', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'V_rms ∝ V₀ and P ∝ V_rms². Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: V_rms ∝ V₀. C1: P ∝ V². A1: A (The r.m.s. voltage doubles and the mean power quadruples).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('e03e0a6d-0bc8-5a70-b330-0d56f5af3c69', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 31, 31, 'An r.m.s. current of 3.0 A flows through a 20 Ω resistor for 1.0 hour. Calculate the energy dissipated.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.48e5', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P = 3.0² × 20 = 180 W. E = Pt = 180 × 3600 = 6.48 × 10⁵ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: P = I_rms²R. C1: E = Pt. A1: E = 6.48 × 10⁵ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('4831b02a-3505-58d7-998b-3024376f58c7', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 32, 32, 'For a triangular waveform, I_rms = I₀/√3. Calculate the r.m.s. value of a triangular current of peak 6.0 A.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.464', 'A', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'I_rms = 6.0 ÷ √3 = 3.46 A. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: formula. C1: substitution. B1: differs from the sine-wave value 4.24 A. A1: I_rms = 3.46 A.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgebb8bc84-t fgebb8bc84-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgebb8bc84-t">Triangular current</title><desc id="fgebb8bc84-d">Graph of current I against time t for a triangular waveform that rises linearly from 0 to a peak of 6.0 A, falls linearly to −6.0 A and returns to 0 in each period T. Two periods are shown.</desc><line x1="39.8" y1="138" x2="388" y2="138" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="39.8" y1="242" x2="39.8" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,138 385.7,141 385.7,135" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="39.8,24 42.7,30.3 36.8,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="39.8" y1="138" x2="39.8" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="39.8" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="210.9" y1="138" x2="210.9" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="210.9" y1="34" x2="210.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig