('c74b459f-0f0f-5551-8210-e20317d94516', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 38, 38, 'How many times per second does the direction of a 50 Hz alternating current reverse?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '100', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The current reverses twice in each cycle: 2 × 50 = 100 times per second. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: two reversals per cycle. C1: value. A1: reversals = 100.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('8cbc702f-eda1-5be0-93fe-984a99c944c5', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 39, 39, 'A voltmeter connected to the mains reads 230 V. What does this reading represent?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The r.m.s. voltage', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'a.c. meters are calibrated to read r.m.s. values. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: meters read r.m.s. C1: peak is 325 V. A1: C (The r.m.s. voltage).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('0ca9b233-1c3c-5d89-9d6c-739d8324632f', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 40, 40, 'A sinusoidal current of peak 3.0 A flows through an 8.0 Ω resistor. (a) Calculate the maximum instantaneous power. (b) Calculate the mean power. (c) Calculate the r.m.s. current. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '36', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P₀ = I₀²R = 3.0² × 8.0 = 72 W. (b) Mean P = 0.5 × 72 = 36 W. (c) I_rms = 3.0 ÷ √2 = 2.12 A; check I_rms²R = 36 W. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: P₀ = I₀²R. M1: mean = ½P₀. C1: value. C1: I_rms. A1: P = 36 W.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('fee8f1f5-4a19-5791-a547-801316251081', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '521788f0-c0f7-5ff9-94e2-bd6a8cd8daac', 'a-level', '27.1', '9702 coursebook 27.1 · Alternating current and r.m.s. values', 41, 41, 'A voltage is +6.0 V for 2.0 ms and −2.0 V for the next 2.0 ms, repeating. (a) Calculate the mean voltage. (b) Calculate the mean of V². (c) Calculate the r.m.s. voltage. (d) Calculate the mean power it delivers to a 10 Ω resistor. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.472', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Mean = (6.0 − 2.0) ÷ 2 = 2.0 V. (b) Mean of V² = (6.0² + 2.0²) ÷ 2 = 20 V². (c) V_rms = √20 = 4.47 V. (d) P = V_rms²/R = 20 ÷ 10 = 2.0 W. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: mean. C1: mean of squares. C1: square root. C1: P = V_rms²/R. B1: r.m.s. ≠ mean. A1: V_rms = 4.47 V.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fga2cf6775-t fga2cf6775-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fga2cf6775-t">Asymmetric square wave</title><desc id="fga2cf6775-d">Graph of voltage against time in ms: the voltage is +6.0 V from 0 to 2.0 ms, −2.0 V from 2.0 ms to 4.0 ms, and the pattern repeats to 8.0 ms.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="117.6" y1="34" x2="117.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="205.7" y1="34" x2="205.7" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="293.9" y1="34" x2="293.9" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="34" x2="382" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);st