('ccea4d23-287c-5a8d-acbc-30b8f4558b06', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '53ce2bac-1ccd-4752-8293-357079212398', 'a-level', '27.3', '9702 coursebook 27.3 · Power and alternating current', 47, 47, 'The instantaneous power in a resistor on a sinusoidal supply is P = P₀ sin² ωt, with P₀ = 2.0 kW. (a) State the mean power. (b) Calculate the instantaneous power at t = T/6. (c) Explain why P is never negative. Give only the answer to (b), in kW.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.5 kW', 'kW', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P₀/2 = 1.0 kW. (b) At t = T/6, ωt = 60°: P = 2.0 × sin² 60° = 2.0 × 0.750 = 1.50 kW. (c) sin² is never negative, because P = I²R. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: mean P₀/2. M1: ωt = 60°. C1: sin² 60°. C1: value. B1: I² ≥ 0. A1: P = 1.50 kW.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg8b824fdc-t fg8b824fdc-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg8b824fdc-t">P = P₀ sin² ωt</title><desc id="fg8b824fdc-d">Graph of P = P₀ sin² ωt against time t over one period T, with P₀ = 2.0 kW: P rises from 0 to 2.0 kW at T/4, falls to 0 at T/2 and repeats. A dashed vertical line marks t = T/6.</desc><line x1="33" y1="242" x2="388" y2="242" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="33" y1="242" x2="33" y2="28" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="392,242 385.7,245 385.7,239" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><polygon points="33,24 36,30.3 30.1,30.3" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><line x1="33" y1="242" x2="33" y2="246" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="33" y="254" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0</text><line x1="91.2" y1