29.4 368,230.8 368.9,232.1 369.8,233.4 370.7,234.6 371.5,235.6 372.4,236.7 373.3,237.6 374.1,238.4 375,239.2 375.9,239.8 376.8,240.4 377.6,240.9 378.5,241.3 379.4,241.6 380.3,241.8 381.1,242 382,242" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'Graph of P = P₀ sin² ωt against time t over one period T, with P₀ = 2.0 kW: P rises from 0 to 2.0 kW at T/4, falls to 0 at T/2 and repeats. A dashed vertical line marks t = T/6.'),
('293d46e9-1cf4-5e54-8dca-a2f279d32ebe', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '53ce2bac-1ccd-4752-8293-357079212398', 'a-level', '27.3', '9702 coursebook 27.3 · Power and alternating current', 48, 48, 'Heaters rated 1.0 kW and 2.0 kW (each at 230 V r.m.s.) are connected in series to a 230 V r.m.s. supply. (a) Calculate the resistance of each. (b) Calculate the current. (c) Calculate the power in the 1.0 kW heater. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '444.4', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) R₁ = 230² ÷ 1000 = 52.9 Ω; R₂ = 230² ÷ 2000 = 26.4 Ω. (b) I = 230 ÷ (52.90 + 26.45) = 2.90 A. (c) P = (2.899)² × 52.90 = 444 W. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: resistances. C1: series current. C1: P = I²R. B1: less than rated power. A1: P = 444 W.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 182" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg5629cc5b-t fg5629cc5b-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg5629cc5b-t">Heaters in series</title><desc id="fg5629cc5b-d">Circuit: a 230 V r.m.s. supply in series with a heater rated 1.0 kW and a heater rated 2.0 kW.</desc><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="70" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="106" y1="48" x2="150" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="70" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="88" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.0 kW heater</text><line x1="150" y1="48" x2="194" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="230" y1="48" x2="274" y2="48" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="194" y="41" width="36" height="14" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="212" y="24" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.0 kW heater</text><line x1="26" y1="142" x2="138" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="162" y1="142" x2="274" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="150" cy="142" r="12" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><path d="M143,142 q3.5,-7 7,0 t7,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="150" y="166" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">230 V r.m.s.</text><line x1="26" y1="48" x2="26" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="274" y1="48" x2="274" y2="142" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></svg>', 'Circuit: a 230 V r.m.s. supply in series with a heater rated 1.0 kW and a heater rated 2.0 kW.'),
('569229d0-25a6-598d-819e-70da7d7c0f6e', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '53ce2bac-1ccd-4752-8293-357079212398', 'a-level', '27.3', '9702 coursebook 27.3 · Power and alternating current', 49, 49, 'A transmission line operates at 400 kV r.m.s. (a) Calculate the peak voltage, in kV. (b) Calculate the peak-to-peak voltage. (c) Explain why the insulators must be designed for the peak, not the r.m.s., value. Give only the answer to (a).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '565.7 kV', 'kV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) V₀ = 400 × √2 = 566 kV. (b) V_pp = 2 × 565.7 = 1130 kV. (c) Breakdown happens at the largest instantaneous voltage. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: × √2. C1: value. C1: × 2. B1: breakdown at peak. A1: V₀ = 566 kV.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('c7d83b4a-25ab-54b6-8306-49432b59927a', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '53ce2bac-1ccd-4752-8293-357079212398', 'a-level', '27.3', '9702 coursebook 27.3 · Power and alternating current', 50, 50, 'A sinusoidal current of peak 2.0 A flows through a 50 Ω resistor for 10 minutes. (a) Calculate the mean power. (b) Calculate the energy dissipated. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '60000', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = ½ × 2.0² × 50 = 100 W. (b) E = 100 × 600 = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: ½I₀²R. C1: value. C1: t in s. C1: E = Pt. A1: E = 6.0 × 10⁴ J.', 5, NULL, NULL)
INSERT INTO problem_options (id, problem_id, option_text, option_letter, is_correct, "order") VALUES
('8e5be8b8-018f-53fe-a5a0-bfe691d11dfb', 'a470bb24-26ca-503a-af48-ed803041b14b', 'Mean power = peak power', 'A', false, 0),
('a17e9bdd-19a1-5fd8-b4a1-bfe5af6a8d1b', 'a470bb24-26ca-503a-af48-ed803041b14b', 'Mean power = 2 × peak power', 'B', false, 1),
('23c7257e-bf63-5d79-b2ea-07b286854d44', 'a470bb24-26ca-503a-af48-ed803041b14b', 'Mean power = ½ × peak power', 'C', true, 2),
('f66088d4-99b9-5f0a-823b-e00856e4ea5c', 'a470bb24-26ca-503a-af48-ed803041b14b', 'Mean power = peak power ÷ √2', 'D', false, 3),
('2818d8f7-dad8-5a0b-b7ae-04493e39226a', '31f960c8-6eac-5297-8016-e80efec498d5', 'V_rms²/R', 'A', true, 0),
('70004084-0f04-5471-b075-32b42f8d5d9a', '31f960c8-6eac-5297-8016-e80efec498d5', 'V₀²/R', 'B', false, 1),
