troke:none"/><circle cx="227" cy="200" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><circle cx="157" cy="120" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><circle cx="297" cy="120" r="3.2" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/><text x="309" y="107" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">+</text><text x="144" y="107" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">−</text></svg>', 'Circuit: a 6.0 V r.m.s. a.c. supply connected to two opposite corners of a bridge of four diodes, with the load resistor between the other two corners.'),
('491027ae-a457-551d-b9a8-4488bdae6020', '94fd6e1e-0c0d-4097-b9f4-51457e7b9b07', '71ba01f7-9a9e-5018-974d-21999c276ce5', 'a-level', '27.4', '9702 coursebook 27.4 · Rectification and smoothing', 50, 50, 'A smoothed full-wave rectifier on a 50 Hz supply has a ripple of 0.50 V. The same circuit is then used on a 60 Hz supply. (a) Explain why the ripple changes. (b) Calculate the new ripple, using ΔV ∝ time between peaks. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.4167', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) At 60 Hz the peaks come more often, so the capacitor discharges for less time. (b) ΔV = 0.50 × 50 ÷ 60 = 0.417 V. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: shorter gap between peaks. M1: ΔV ∝ 1/f. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: ΔV = 0.417 V.', 5, NULL, NULL)
